{"schema_version": 1, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T17:58:05.376083", "run_id": "ab-real_llm-offset_scorer-s1-B-20261020175536", "task_id": "offset_scorer", "seed": 1, "iteration": 2, "mode": "meta_controller_on", "judge": {"judge_type": "llm", "model_name": "openai/gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07", "provider": "openai", "model": "gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07", "call_args": {}, "max_parse_retries": 1, "prompt_version": "v1", "diagnostics": false, "max_ops_in_prompt": 1}, "validator": {"name": "offset_scorer_truth", "higher_is_better": true, "top_k": 5, "ranking": "population order (system ranking)", "draws": {"validation": 48, "test": 256, "calibration": 64}, "synthetic": true}, "acceptance_rule": {"split": "validation", "seeds": [2000, 2001], "min_improvement": 0.02, "rule": "accept iff all trials ran, no constraint violation, budget not exceeded, and mean(candidate) - mean(baseline) > 0 and >= min_improvement"}, "accepted_state_version_before": 0, "decision": {"failure_location": "implementation", "root_cause_hypothesis": "The quality_score scorer (or its upstream scaling) is producing values outside the documented [0,1] range (observed mean \u2248 1.8897, best = 1.9000), so the scoring implementation is incorrect or mis-scaled.", "intervention_target": "implementation", "rationale": "Analyzer reports quality_score values violating the documented [0,1] range (mean ~1.8897, best 1.9000). Until the scorer is corrected, quality-based evaluations are untrustworthy and may mislead optimization. Enforcing the documented range will prevent the optimizer from using invalid values and force reevaluation/fixing.", "proposed_change": {"objective_name": "quality_score", "action": "sanitize_outputs", "valid_range": [0.0, 1.0]}, "expected_benefit_hypothesis": "Any non-finite or out-of-range quality_score values will be treated as failed evaluations, preventing the optimizer from relying on invalid quality magnitudes. This reduces the risk of optimization being driven by mis-scaled quality values and buys time to correct the scorer implementation; population statistics should then be recomputed with only valid quality evaluations.", "validation_plan_suggested_by_judge": "After this change, re-run the evaluation pass for the current population and subsequent candidates; verify that no quality_score values outside [0,1] are accepted, and confirm population summary no longer reports mean >1.0. If many evaluations fail, trigger implementer action to correct the quality_score scorer and then remove the sanitizer.", "self_reported_confidence_uncalibrated": 0.7, "target_assessment": null}, "notes": [], "judge_raw_output": "The Analyzer shows a clear implementation-level problem: quality_score outputs are outside the documented [0,1] range (mean \u2248 1.8897, best = 1.9000 vs documented [0,1]). This indicates a scorer/scaling bug and the optimizer is currently using invalid quality values. The safest immediate, bounded intervention is to stop accepting out-of-range quality_score values so the optimizer no longer trusts them while the scorer is fixed.\n\n<decision>{\n  \"failure_location\": \"implementation\",\n  \"root_cause_hypothesis\": \"The quality_score scorer (or its upstream scaling) is producing values outside the documented [0,1] range (observed mean \u2248 1.8897, best = 1.9000), so the scoring implementation is incorrect or mis-scaled.\",\n  \"intervention_target\": \"implementation\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Analyzer reports quality_score values violating the documented [0,1] range (mean ~1.8897, best 1.9000). Until the scorer is corrected, quality-based evaluations are untrustworthy and may mislead optimization. Enforcing the documented range will prevent the optimizer from using invalid values and force reevaluation/fixing.\",\n  \"proposed_change\": {\n    \"objective_name\": \"quality_score\",\n    \"action\": \"sanitize_outputs\",\n    \"valid_range\": [0.0, 1.0]\n  },\n  \"expected_benefit\": \"Any non-finite or out-of-range quality_score values will be 